<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaska</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaska was an ancient Indian grammarian and author of the Nirukta, the oldest surviving treatise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> etymology and the interpretation of Vedic words. Usually dated to around the 5th century BCE, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>, he discussed how words derive from verbal roots. In passing he notes that the Kambojas used a distinct word for going, often taken as evidence of their Iranian speech.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yāska's exact century is difficult to determine, and the opinions of scholars vary. Tradition makes him the author of the Nirukta and of the Nighantu, the oldest proto-thesaurus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He is thought to have succeeded Śākaṭāyana, an old grammarian and expositor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> whom his text mentions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a>, who came after him, cites at least ten earlier grammarians and linguists. Nirukta, or etymology, became one of the six Vedāṅgas, the limbs of the Veda and compulsory subjects of Vedic scholarship in ancient India.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Nirukta has three parts: the Naighantuka, a collection of synonyms; the Naigama, words peculiar to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>; and the Daivata, words relating to deities and sacrifices. It sets out rules for forming words from roots and affixes and a glossary of irregular words, and it formed the basis for later lexicons and dictionaries.</p><p>Yāska defined four main categories of words: nāma, nouns; ākhyāta, verbs; upasarga, pre-verbs or prefixes; and nipāta, particles. A verb is a word in which a process (bhāva) predominates, as in vrajati, walks, and a noun one in which a thing (sattva) predominates. Where a process is treated as a whole from start to finish, he held, a verbal noun such as vrajyā, a walk, is used. Scholars have related these ideas to the modern notion of grammatical aspect. Following the lost text of Śākaṭāyana, he also defended the view that most nouns derive from verbs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Yāska is widely regarded as the precursive founder of the discipline that became etymology in both the East and the West. His view that words are the main carriers of meaning was fiercely debated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for twelve centuries. On one side stood the Nairuktas, atomists who followed the Nirukta and took words as the primary elements of a sentence; on the other the Vaiyākarans, grammarians following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a>, holists who held that the sentence comes first and words are reached only by analysis. Philosophers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyaya">Nyaya</a> and Mimamsa schools and Buddhist thinkers took part in the debate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yāska” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Yaska</b> (4th century BCE) was an early commentator, and in his Nirukta he holds that the text can be read in three ways: by rite, by deity and by the soul.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vedic etymologist <b>Yaska</b> noted that they used an Iranian word for to go, and many scholars see them as an Iranian-speaking people.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamboja">Kamboja</a></li></ul></section>
